@AGENTS.md

The rules in `AGENTS.md` apply to any coding agent working in this repo
(Claude, Codex, Cursor, Gemini, whatever). It is the single source of truth
— recent Cursor versions read it directly, and this rule pulls it in for the
versions that don't. Don't duplicate rules back into this file; add them to
`AGENTS.md` so they reach every tool, not just this one.

This file holds the things that genuinely are Cursor-specific.

## Read the rule file before touching the area

`AGENTS.md` links out to `docs/rules/*.md`. The summary in `AGENTS.md` is not
enough on its own — open the linked file before you write code that touches
database access, migrations, auth, state management, error handling,
components, code style, code quality, commits, PRs, or deployment.

## This is not the Next.js in your training data

Next.js 16 and React 19 are pinned here, with real breaking changes vs. the
v13/14 App Router conventions. Read `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` before
writing anything that touches routing, caching, or data fetching. In short:
every dynamic API (`params`, `searchParams`, `cookies()`, `headers()`) is
async, caching is opt-in via `'use cache'`, and Turbopack is the default
bundler.

React Compiler runs with `panicThreshold: 'all_errors'` — a bailout fails the
build rather than silently skipping optimization. Fix the underlying issue
instead of routing around the Compiler.

## Session logs

`.claude-logs/` is tracked, not gitignored — the transcript for the session
that produced a change belongs on the branch that ships it. Cursor doesn't
write one automatically, so there's usually nothing to stage from a Cursor
session. If you do save a transcript or working log, put it there with the
same `YYYY-MM-DD_HH-MM-SS_<id>.md` naming and stage it immediately before
`git push`, separately from the code commit.

## Workflows

Cursor has no skills mechanism, but the repo's workflows are plain Markdown
at `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` — read the file and follow its steps
rather than improvising. The ones that matter most day to day:

- `local-review` — the full local gate (lint, typecheck, format, env check,
  standards check, unit tests, build) plus a rules pass. Run it before committing, not after
  CI fails; every push is a real CI bill.
- `typesafe` — audits the type assertions this branch introduced.
- `e2e-review` — before opening a PR for a UI/flow change.
- `preview-acceptance-testing` — the pre-merge gate against the PR's live
  Vercel preview.
- `sql-preview`, `seed-for-pr`, `local-feature-testing`, `dogfood`,
  `create-issue` — day-to-day helpers.
- `template-setup` / `dev-onboarding` — the two one-time setup paths.

## Commands

The `cicd` workflow — review the diff, fix what matters, open or update the
PR, watch CI to green, work the review comments — has committed definitions
at `.claude/commands/cicd.md` (Claude Code) and `.codex/commands/cicd.md`
(Codex). Same checklist, different tool dialects. Read either and follow it;
keep every copy in sync when any of them changes.
